dows users use to develop Dyalog APL locally on their computers. As the “non-Windows users” segment of Dyalog’s user base has always been comparatively small, ","position":{"start":{"line":8,"column":1},"end":{"line":8,"column":1}},"key":"wyUeKzsDMj"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Ride","position":{"start":{"line":8,"column":1},"end":{"line":8,"column":1}},"key":"kra8jZLTUe"},{"type":"text","value":" occasionally feels not entirely comfortable on macOS or linux. Especially on macOS, it doesn’t always respect the established OS conventions, particularly when it comes to how it handles multiple windows.","position":{"start":{"line":8,"column":1},"end":{"line":8,"column":1}},"key":"Z9medUb50c"}],"key":"YrqOAXeMX1"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The reason it’s like this is that the “classic” model for the Windows IDE is that it opens separate floating windows for everything, and this doesn’t really fit with the application model of Electron -- or indeed macOS, for that matter. If you’re a classic-mode Dyalog user on Windows and you need ","position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"key":"WBjrYsbWwo"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Ride","position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"key":"cFQQKj9xqS"},{"type":"text","value":" to debug a remote session, the model feels comfortable, and this is the root of the uncanny valley that makes ","position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"key":"zRjF0snP01"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Ride","position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"key":"zrYeEdEoe6"},{"type":"text","value":" feel a bit awkward. Multiple sessions in ","position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"key":"ZMntcSeDRB"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Ride","position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"key":"ysFaZcVgWU"},{"type":"text","value":" are completely separate application processes, and on macOS, you’ll then see many ","position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"key":"ymeYBcuTGS"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Ride","position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"key":"Yfc7jb0kqs"},{"type":"text","value":" icons in your dock, instead of a single icon which gathers its session windows like you’d probably expect.","position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"key":"vkW03nZuz0"}],"key":"sPZhGFS6V1"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":12,"column":1},"end":{"line":12,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Other issues -- that we ","position":{"start":{"line":12,"column":1},"end":{"line":12,"column":1}},"key":"a7U17xV397"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":12,"column":1},"end":{"line":12,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"can","position":{"start":{"line":12,"column":1},"end":{"line":12,"column":1}},"key":"kHOmXUcfqE"}],"key":"IhJqVqY0W0"},{"type":"text","value":" partly address -- are for example the out of box configuration settings that are both hard to discover and not set to the expected platform defaults. The other aspect is the philosophy: ","position":{"start":{"line":12,"column":1},"end":{"line":12,"column":1}},"key":"qGIsfCPe1Y"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Ride","position":{"start":{"line":12,"column":1},"end":{"line":12,"column":1}},"key":"HQVYfSyXmg"},{"type":"text","value":" ","position":{"start":{"line":12,"column":1},"end":{"line":12,"column":1}},"key":"hheSYCgXIO"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":12,"column":1},"end":{"line":12,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"looks","position":{"start":{"line":12,"column":1},"end":{"line":12,"column":1}},"key":"m8xv5HPGC1"}],"key":"SX6S6cEpAa"},{"type":"text","value":" much like any other editor/IDE you will be familiar with, but is better seen