to the “dumb display tool” idea, is actually mostly the interpreter itself dictating the behaviour. The session is really an active canvas, and does not at all behave like the shell interface it looks like. If you expect to be able to use the arrow keys to recall your session history, you’ll be surprised to see that instead it moves the cursor up and down your previous output. If you press return, it tries to re-execute whatever line you happen to be on -- including lines that cannot be executed. This plays badly with boxed output, and the beginner will frequently end up in a state like this:","position":{"start":{"line":14,"column":1},"end":{"line":14,"column":1}},"key":"EyNATbmnnq"}],"key":"IBjhKqqtER"},{"type":"image","url":"/learnapl/build/ride-1-5435e5daac63765ab635f2910ca744d9.png","alt":"ride1","position":{"start":{"line":16,"column":1},"end":{"line":16,"column":1}},"key":"WR3J8Kz4i3","urlSource":"./IMG/ride-1.png","urlOptimized":"/learnapl/build/ride-1-5435e5daac63765ab635f2910ca744d9.webp"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":18,"column":1},"end":{"line":18,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"A long, boxed output, and you tried moving up a few lines and pressed return, expecting shell behaviour, but got a canvas. Perhaps you then tried to clear the screen from all the junk output with a ","position":{"start":{"line":18,"column":1},"end":{"line":18,"column":1}},"key":"lzAthmPUMe"},{"type":"keyboard","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ctrl","key":"HmRgz2JVjg"}],"key":"recGPosHOv"},{"type":"text","value":"-","position":{"start":{"line":18,"column":1},"end":{"line":18,"column":1}},"key":"JOHX95bvgs"},{"type":"keyboard","children":[{"type":"text","value":"l","key":"cdXH2i2AGn"}],"key":"JCZb6a9wCy"},{"type":"text","value":", but that had no effect. The ability to re-evaluate lines in the output flow is something APLers love and see as a feature. It takes a while to adapt to, if you used to normal editors, shells and REPLs. You ","positio